    settle:
      'The injectivity itself is a one-line consequence of Hilbert 90; the question is whether the determination of a tannakian category, or a gerbe with abelian band, by these numerical data has been stated. Look in J. Giraud, Cohomologie non abélienne (1971), IV.3, on gerbes with abelian band; in Saavedra, Catégories tannakiennes (LNM 265, 1972), chapter III; in Deligne–Milne (LNM 900, 1982), § 3; for the general case, in J.-J. Sansuc, « Groupe de Brauer et arithmétique des groupes algébriques linéaires sur un corps de nombres » (J. reine angew. Math. 327, 1981), on H² of tori; and, for the finite-group side, in P. Etingof and S. Gelaki, « Isocategorical groups » (IMRN 2001). All are cited from memory. If the rank-one case, or a general criterion containing it, is there, mark matched. No web search was available for this pass (2026-09-23), so nothing has been read.',
    reach: 'beyond',
    theory: "stacks, gerbes, Picard categories",
    onPage: 'partial',
    lean: { file: "lean/Grothendieck/Folder10.lean", found: "Partly proved (torus_H2_injective, norm_one_torus_H2_injective, norm_one_torus_H2_injective_quadratic). The cohomological step of Exemple 1 on page 65 is proved at a finite Galois level. Take L/K finite Galois, Γ = Gal(L/K), and χ : Γ → {±1} the sign of a quadratic Z ⊆ L (quadChar). For any Γ-module M, with M_χ the χ-twist and Ind M = M × M where Γ swaps the factors outside Ker χ, the sequence 0 → M_χ → Ind M → M → 0 given by x ↦ (x, −x) and (x, y) ↦ x + y is exact. When H¹(Γ, M) = 0, the long exact sequence makes H²(Γ, M_χ) → H²(Γ, Ind M) injective. For M = Lˣ this is 1 → G(L) → (Z ⊗_K L)ˣ → Lˣ → 1, written in the basis of the two idempotents, with the norm on the right. Mathlib's Hilbert 90 (H1ofAutOnUnitsUnique) gives the injectivity of H²(Gal(L/K), G(L)) → H²(Gal(L/K), (Z ⊗_K L)ˣ). Through Shapiro (indShapiro, an explicit isomorphism of Ind M with the coinduced module), the composite H²(Gal(L/K), G(L)) → H²(Gal(L/Z), Lˣ) is injective too. This settles the statement Ker(H²(k, G) → H²(k, Z*)) = 0 of the claim at every finite level L ⊇ Z, for every χ, including the split case. The page's own special case is the rank-one torus of Exemple 1. Beyond mathlib: Lean does not identify Ind Lˣ with (Z ⊗_K L)ˣ (it takes the idempotent model as the definition), nor H²(Gal(L/Z), Lˣ) with Br(L/Z). It does not pass to the limit over L to get H²(k, G) → Br(Z). The tannakian conclusion, that the numerical character determines the category, is not formalised: it needs Tannakian categories, gerbes and bands, none of them in mathlib." },
