    claim:
      'The folder asserts, without proof, that for P^n ⊃ X^r ⊃ Y^m with r > m > 0, the vanishing H^p(X − Y, F) = 0 for p ≥ m and every coherent F is equivalent — through H^p_Y(X, F) → H^p(X, F) being bijective for p > m and surjective for p = m, and a duality with Ext^q(−, Ω^r) on X and on its formal completion X̂ along Y — to H^q(X, F) → H^q(X̂, F̂) being bijective for q < r − m and injective for q = r − m for F locally free, each step reduced to twists O(n).',
    basis:
      'Page 2 is a chart of boxed statements joined by vertical equivalences, one of them labelled « dualité », with a right-hand column reducing each to O(n), n grand, and an N.B. dispensing with the case p = m because H^m(X, O(−n)) = 0 for n large; page 4 writes H^p_Y(X, F) = lim Ext^p(P; O_{Y_n}, F), H^p(X − Y, F) = lim Ext^p(P; J^n, F) and the dual inverse limit on the completion; page 7 boxes H^i(P̂, Ω^r_P(−n)) = 0 for 0 < i < r − m with the margin « ⟺ P − X est de dim. coh. r − m ». Under the chart: the bound of page 2’s first display is illegible, « réduit à » in the right-hand column is uncertain, « [m = dim Y] » stands between two illegible stretches, and the diagonal N.B. at the foot is legible only in fragments.',
    ours:
      'The reading names no published statement for the chart: it gives it as one chain of equivalences, states the duality as a perfect pairing, and footnotes the fifth box as « la forme sous laquelle l’énoncé est aujourd’hui le plus reconnaissable » without naming it. This pass reads the chart, from memory, as Hartshorne’s comparison between the cohomological dimension of X − Y and the formal completion along Y (Ample Subvarieties of Algebraic Varieties, 1970, chapter III, for X non-singular projective), the page’s m playing the part of its q, and records the entry as a guard to be marked matched rather than as a candidate. The passage between the first three boxes (the exact sequence, then the duality taken as given) is proved in lean/Grothendieck/Folder125.lean; the formal completion is not. It also reads the folder differently from the reading in four places: Ω^r_{X/k} is a dualizing sheaf only for X smooth, which neither the page nor the reading states; the reading’s single notation — P = P^n, X of dimension r — does not fit pages 4 and 7, which write P^r ⊃ X ⊃ Y and Ω^r_P, so that there r is the dimension of the ambient space; the reading’s footnote that every coherent sheaf is a quotient of a sum of O(n), n large, should read O(−n), as page 2’s N.B. has it; and its footnote to page 7 folds page 2’s complement X − Y into page 7’s P − X, which are different statements. Page 7’s second boxed line, H^r(P, Ω^r(−n)) ≅ H^r(P̂, Ω̂^r(−n)), cannot hold as transcribed when the hat is a completion along a closed subset of smaller dimension — its right side is then zero — and the reading reproduces it without comment. Written on Opus 5.5 against a reading made on Opus 5.',
    literature: [
      "R. Hartshorne, « Cohomology of non-complete algebraic varieties », Compositio Math. 23 (1971), no. 3, 257-264 (numdam CM_1971__23_3_257_0), read 2026-10-25. § 2, p. 258-259: « By duality we are led to study the cohomology groups H^i(X̂, O(v)) for small i and for all v ∈ Z, where X̂ is the formal completion of X along Y ». Proposition 2.9 (Duality), p. 261, « (See [4, III 3.4]) »: for X = P^n_k, Y closed, U = X − Y, under condition (I*_s) and for every r ≤ s, these are equivalent: (i) H^i(U, F) = 0 for all i ≥ n − r and all coherent F on U; (ii) H^i(U, ω) = 0 for all i ≥ n − r, ω = Ω^n_{X/k}; (iii) the maps H^i(X, O_X) → H^i(X̂, O_X̂) are isomorphisms for i < r. Reference [4] is Ample Subvarieties of Algebraic Varieties, LNM 156 (1970). This is the chart's equivalence with ambient space P^n, the comparison stated through the twists of O, and without the injectivity clause.",
      "R. Hartshorne, Ample Subvarieties of Algebraic Varieties, LNM 156, Springer, 1970, chapter III, § 3, Theorem 3.4. Only the first two pages of chapter III (Springer page-one preview, read 2026-10-25) were read: p. 82, « In §3 we apply duality to relate the cohomology of X̂ to the cohomology of U = X − Y. Thus we establish some relations between the dimension of Y and the cohomology of U ». The text of Theorem 3.4 was not read: the book is paywalled and zbMATH (Zbl 0208.48901) has no review.",
      "M. Halic, « Subvarieties with q-ample normal bundle and q-ample subvarieties », arXiv:1511.07524, read 2026-10-25: Proposition 7.4 and its proof (p. 20-21) cite « [27, Theorem III.3.4(b)] » (27 = LNM 156) for: X non-singular, cd(X∖Z) < dim X − p implies that for every locally free F, H^t(X, F) → H^t(X̂_Z, F) is an isomorphism for t ≤ p − 1 and injective for t = p. With dim X = r and p = r − m this is exactly the direction « H^i(X − Y, F) = 0 for i ≥ m ⟹ bijective for q < r − m, injective for q = r − m » of the folder's chart. A proof in § 1, just before § 2.1 « Cohomology of the complement », also uses « formal duality (cf. [27, Theorem III.3.3]) » and « [27, Lemma III.3.2] » (reduction to G = ω_X ⊗ F^∨, F locally free).",
      "M. Halic, « Vector bundles on projective varieties which split along q-ample subvarieties », arXiv:1410.1207, read 2026-10-25: proof of the lemma with item (ii) (« cd(X ∖ Y_o) ⩽ dim X − 3 implies (cf. [15, Ch. III, Theorem 3.4]) that for any locally free sheaf F on X the restriction H^i(X, F) → H^i(X̂_{Y_o}, F̂) is an isomorphism for i = 0, 1 »), consistent with the same indexing.",
      "J. C. Ottem, « Ample subvarieties and q-ample divisors », arXiv:1105.2500 (Adv. Math. 2012), Corollary 5.6(iv) and its proof, read 2026-10-25: for Y non-singular ample of dimension s, H^i(X, F) → H^i(X̂, F) is an isomorphism for i < s and injective for i = s, deduced from cd(X − Y) = codim Y − 1 with proofs referred to « Hartshorne's book [13, III.3.4, IV.1.1, V.2.1] »; C. C. Lau, « On nef subvarieties », arXiv:1609.07797, Theorem 2.10(4), read 2026-10-25, same statement for F locally free, cited to « [Har70, Chapter III, Theorem 3.4] ».",
      'R. Hartshorne, Ample Subvarieties of Algebraic Varieties (LNM 156, 1970), chapter III, Theorem 3.4 — not read; seen only as cited by M. Halic, « Vector bundles on projective varieties which split along q-ample subvarieties », arXiv:1410.1207, and « Subvarieties with q-ample normal bundle and q-ample subvarieties », arXiv:1511.07524 (Prop. 5.5), read 2026-10-11: for X smooth projective and F locally free, a bound cd(X ∖ Y) ≤ dim X − 2 (resp. − 3) makes H^i(X, F) → H^i(X̂_Y, F̂) an isomorphism for i = 0 (resp. i = 0, 1); Halic notes that smoothness is used only through Serre duality (Hartshorne’s proof of Theorem 3.3). That is the direction « first box ⟹ fifth box » of the chart, in Hartshorne’s indexing; whether Theorem 3.4 states the equivalence was not seen',
      'Google Scholar, 2026-10-11: « Ample subvarieties » Hartshorne + cohomological dimension + formal completion + locally free — Hartshorne–Speiser 1977, Speiser 1978 and 1980, Ogus 1975, Bădescu 1993 and the Halic papers; only the Halic papers and the abstract page of Speiser 1980 were opened',
    ],
