    lean: { file: "lean/Grothendieck/Folder37.lean", found: "The equivalence (i) <=> (ii) of page 22, as formalised in `problem8`, is false, and `not_problem8 : ¬ problem8` is proved in lean/Grothendieck/Folder37.lean (axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound only). The counterexample is a small category C = CatY: a small model of the finitely generated presheaves on the category D with objects top and pt n (n in N) and one arrow pt n -> top for each n. C is closed in presheaves under finite colimits and retracts, so it has finite colimits and is idempotent complete. Condition (ii) holds for it: in C a finite family is effective epimorphic exactly when it is jointly surjective as presheaves (one direction by descent along chosen preimages, the other by testing against the finitely generated presheaf B glued to itself along the image), which gives precoherence; and every sheaf for the coherent topology sends finite colimits of C to limits (separate along the representables generating the source, then chase the zigzags of the colimit of sets), so the Yoneda functor into coherent sheaves preserves finite colimits. These facts are proved for any category with a fully faithful functor onto the finitely generated presheaves, so they apply to C and to its Karoubi envelope. But Ind C is not balanced, so it is no Grothendieck topos: Y∞ (two points over top, glued over every pt n) is finitely generated but not finitely presented, being the union of the Y N (glued only over pt n with n < N); the subpresheaf Q of y(Y∞) of maps factoring through some Y N is the ind-object colim y(Y N), and its inclusion into y(Y∞) is a monomorphism and an epimorphism in Ind C (Y 0 -> Y∞ is an epimorphism of C and Ind.yoneda preserves epimorphisms) but not an isomorphism, since no Y∞ -> Y N -> Y∞ is the identity. Mathematically the direction (i) => (ii) still looks right (the finitely presentable objects of a locally finitely presentable topos are compact, which gives precoherence and the comparison lemma); the gap is in (ii) => (i): coherent sheaves on C' can be fewer than the finite-limit-preserving presheaves when objects of C' are compact without being finitely presented in the coherent topos." },
