      'The folder axiomatises « polynomial categories » — a category 𝐏 with finite products, a wide additive subcategory 𝐏₁ of degree-one maps, and a grading 𝐏(X, Y) = ⊕ 𝐏_n(X, Y) by homogeneity under scalars, with composition additive in the outer variable only and degrees multiplying — and asserts that the strict ones are equivalent to « ⊗-pd-categories »: additive symmetric monoidal categories with an exponential graded functor Γ* (Γ*(X ⊕ Y) ≅ Γ*X ⊗ Γ*Y) and maps α_{p,q} : Γ^{pq} → Γ^p Γ^q satisfying associativity and unit conditions, the passage back being 𝐏_p(X, Y) = Hom(Γ^p X, Y).',
    basis:
      'Pages 74–76 give axioms a)–g) and take the degree decomposition as structure after a struck lemma; pages 77–80 define strict tensor products, Γ^n by representability, derive the exponential formula and the α_{p,q} with their associativity square; pages 81–82 build 𝐏 from a ⊗-pd-category with composition g ∘ Γ^q(f) ∘ α_{q,p}; page 83 says « On vérifie alors les axiomes a) à f) » and « Les deux notions sont donc équivalentes ». The phrase on p. 83 saying what is verified about strict tensor products carries \\ill{} and \\uncertain{} words; page 76 has two nearly illegible margin notes, one beginning « Oublié : ».',
    ours:
      'The equivalence is asserted on p. 83 and not proved; the reading says so. The edition corrects id_Y to id_X in the action μ_n (p. 74), the index of the vertical arrow in the associativity square (p. 80), and reads « applications bil. » (p. 82, \\uncertain{}) as linear in the outer variable only. The reading also notes that the page refers to axioms a)–f) while p. 76 lists a)–g). The match of the folder’s final question with the Friedlander–Suslin definition of strict polynomial functors is a match, recorded in the reading, and is not this claim.',
    literature: [
      "E. Dotto, K. Moi, I. Patchkoria, « Witt vectors, polynomial maps, and real topological Hochschild homology », arXiv:1901.02195 (2019; v2 2020) — §1.1, Prop. 1.4, Def. 1.6, Prop. 1.7, Prop. 1.8 (read) — defines a « µ-filtered comonad » T_n with Δ_{n,m}: T_{nm} → T_n ∘ T_m, coassociativity and unit squares, and its Kleisli category with composition g ∘ T_m(f) ∘ Δ_{m,n}; main example T_n = ∏_{i≤n} Γ^i on rings, Δ_{n,m} classifying the composite of universal polynomial laws (Roby); Prop. 1.8: this Kleisli category is equivalent to the category of rings and multiplicative polynomial laws of finite degree. This is the composition rule and the α_{p,q} structure of the folder (pp. 80–82), in one concrete case, filtered rather than graded, with no abstract axiomatisation and no converse.",
      "R. Blute, R. Cockett, R. Seely, « Cartesian differential storage categories », Theory Appl. Categ. 30 (2015), no. 18, 620–686 (arXiv:1405.6973) — Def. 2.1 (systems of linear maps), Thm 2.29, Prop. 3.16 and Prop. 3.17 (read) — a Cartesian category with a system of linear maps that is classified (storage) and has tensorial representation of bilinear maps has its linear maps forming a tensor storage category (comonad S with Seely isomorphisms S(X×Y) ≅ S X ⊗ S Y, S(1) ≅ ⊤), and conversely the coKleisli category of a tensor storage category is such a Cartesian storage category. Same shape as the folder's equivalence (degree-one maps, representability, exponential formula), but ungraded: no decomposition by degree, no Γ^n, no α_{p,q} indexed by (ℕ,·), no requirement that composition be additive in the outer variable.",
      'Two general web searches (2026-10-10) for categories graded by polynomial degree with composition additive in one variable, and for monoidal categories with an exponential divided-power functor defining polynomial morphisms, found no such axiomatisation; they surfaced the categories Γ^d P of strict polynomial functor theory (Krause, « Koszul, Ringel and Serre duality for strict polynomial functors », arXiv:1203.0311; « Generalized polynomial functors », arXiv:2308.16442) and Roby-style polynomial maps (arXiv:1112.0991), none read for this question',
    ],
    status: 'candidate',
    settle:
      "Searched 2026-10-25 with WebSearch (standard and extended) and WebFetch/curl + pdftotext: queries on categories of polynomial maps Hom(Γ^p X, Y) with α: Γ^{pq} → Γ^p Γ^q as graded comonad/Kleisli; « polynomial category » with degree grading and divided powers; graded linear exponential comonads indexed by (ℕ,·) with Seely isomorphisms; Blute–Cockett–Seely storage categories. Read Dotto–Moi–Patchkoria §1.1 and Blute–Cockett–Seely §§2–3 (theorem statements). Search results also surfaced Krause (arXiv:1203.0311), Touzé (math/0507384), arXiv:1503.05108, arXiv:2308.16442 (categories Γ^d P whose Hom is Γ^d Hom, a different construction) and graded-exponential literature (Brunel et al.; arXiv:2303.10586, 2311.15704, 2401.17199: graded comonads with D_{rs} → D_r D_s but contraction D_{r+s} → D_r ⊗ D_s, not a degree-graded exponential formula), none giving the folder's notion. Not read: Roby 1963/1980 themselves (cited through DMP Prop. 1.4: they give Hom(Γ^n A, B) ≅ degree-n laws, not an axiomatics), Bousfield 1967, Fujii–Katsumata–Melliès on graded monads, Garner–Lemay « Cartesian differential categories as skew enriched categories ». Remains: check those, and any graded refinement of storage categories, before calling the axiomatisation or the equivalence absent from print. Closest in print: Two partial matches. Dotto–Moi–Patchkoria (2019) Prop. 1.7–1.8 print the (ℕ,·)-indexed comonad Γ^{nm} → Γ^n Γ^m (in filtered form ∏_{i≤n} Γ^i) with exactly the folder's Kleisli composition, but only for commutative rings and multiplicative polynomial laws, with no axioms for a polynomial category and no equivalence. Blute–Cockett–Seely (2015) Prop. 3.16–3.17 print the abstract equivalence « category with linear maps + representability of linear-from and bilinear maps ⇔ tensor category with a Seely (exponential) comonad », but without any grading by homogeneous degree, without Γ^n or α_{p,q}, and without the additive structure (composition additive in the outer variable, degree-one maps additive) of axioms a)–g).",
    lean: { file: "lean/Grothendieck/Folder137.lean", found: "The core of the equivalence holds, for any degree-one category C, with no additivity, products or tensor product: a ⊗-pd-structure without ⊗ (functors Γⁿ, natural α_{p,q} : Γ^{pq} → Γ^p Γ^q, Γ¹ ≅ id, the associativity square of p. 80 and the unit conditions (**)) is a comonad graded by the multiplicative monoid (ℕ, ·), and the category 𝐏_p(X, Y) = Hom(Γ^p X, Y) with g ∘ Γ^q(f) ∘ α_{q,p} is its Kleisli category: associative, unital, additive in the outer variable when C is preadditive, with 𝐏ₙ(X, −) represented on C by Γⁿ X. Conversely a polynomial category whose degrees are representable (Γⁿ X, γₙ) yields Γⁿ as functors, α_{p,q} by factoring γ_p ∘ γ_q (page 80's « ce qui revient au même »), natural, with the square and (**); everything is read through φ ↦ φ ∘ γₙ, and each identity reduces to associativity of 𝐏. The two passages are inverse: Γ, α, ε are recovered on the nose, and the polynomial category up to an isomorphism compatible with composition, identities and degree-one maps. So the composition rule of p. 82 is forced by representability, and naturality of α is what associativity needs. Not formalised: axioms a)–c) (products, additivity), the multidegree decomposition d) and homogeneity under scalars, f), g) and the half 𝟏 ≅ Γ^q(𝟏) of (***), strict tensor products, the exponential formula Γ*(X ⊕ Y) ≅ Γ*X ⊗ Γ*Y, and the k-module example (Roby)." },
