      'Over a base S, a resolution of the Steinberg map G → T/W that is an isomorphism over G^reg with exceptional locus of codimension ≥ 2 fibre by fibre cannot exist, because its Picard group would be finite modulo Pic(S) while that of the Grothendieck–Springer space G̃ contains the weight lattice with its ample cone. The necessity of the base change T → T/W is obtained here from a Picard-group comparison, not from monodromy.',
    basis:
      'Pages 10–11 prove Pic(Bor) ↪ Pic(G̃) ≅ Pic(G̃^reg) ≅ Pic(G′^reg) by depth and EGA IV 21.4.13. Page 13 d) proves Pic(S) ↪ Pic(G) ↪ Pic(G^reg) with finite cokernel, then concludes « Cela montre qu’on ne peut trouver une résolution X̃ … sans toucher à G^reg ». The words « ne peut » are \\uncertain{} in the transcription, and the sentence breaks off on « … ».',
    ours:
      "The reading adds the hypothesis that S is regular for the finiteness of Coker(Pic(S) → Pic(G^reg)). The page strikes « si S régulier » and infers finiteness from injectivity alone. The reading also writes out the contradiction (rank 0 against the rank-r weight lattice with its ample cone), which the page leaves at « … ». As written, the argument needs X̃ regular, so that Pic(X̃) = Pic(X̃ minus the exceptional locus), and X̃ → G projective, so that it has a relatively ample bundle. Neither assumption is stated on the page. Precise statement fixed by the edition: Let S be regular noetherian and G a split reductive S-group of rank r ≥ 1 with simply connected derived group, χ : G → T/W the Steinberg map. There is no regular scheme X̃ with a projective morphism π : X̃ → G that is an isomorphism over G^reg, whose exceptional locus has codimension ≥ 2 in every fibre over S, and such that χ ∘ π : X̃ → T/W is smooth. To fix the statement the edition keeps the reading’s hypothesis that S is regular and adds the two assumptions its argument uses, X̃ regular and π projective. It adds smoothness of X̃ → T/W, which is what « résolution » of the Steinberg map means. Then the page’s route works: Pic(X̃) = Pic(G^reg) is finite modulo Pic(S), so a relatively ample bundle has a trivial power, π is finite and birational onto the normal G, hence an isomorphism, and G → T/W is not smooth at the unipotent elements. The same conclusion follows more directly from purity of the exceptional locus of a proper birational map to the regular scheme G.",
