    ours:
      'Much of the proof is the edition’s: the case k[ε] of Prop. 1 (i) (the page breaks off after « ε² = 0 »), the count 2(n−1) = n² − n behind « n = 2 », the justification that J is nilpotent, and the whole end of page 18 (dim V = 1), where the page stops at « il faut prouver ». The hypothesis on residue extensions is a marginal addition the reading folds into the statement; the case-split word « n’est pas [radiciel] » on page 14 is \\uncertain{} in the transcription.',
    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder128.lean', found: "Partly: the last assertion in the case A = k only — for a commutative algebra of dimension 2 over any field, the equivalence ideals of A ⊗_k A are exactly 0 and Δ (eq_bot_or_eq_ker), k[ε] failing transitivity and k × k symmetry. The artinian reduction to that case (Prop. 3, Cor. 2 of N° 1) and the colength criterion of Prop. 2 are not formalised. Added 2026-10-25: Partly, now including Prop. 2 in the field case the page reduces to. For A a finite commutative algebra over any field k whose residue fields are all k (every maximal ideal the kernel of a k-point), and any ideal J ⊂ Δ of A ⊗_k A with k → A ⇉ (A ⊗ A)/J exact and Δ ⊂ (δx) + J for every x ∉ k, dim_k A ≤ 2 (Colength.finrank_le_two); if moreover J is an equivalence ideal and dim_k A = 2, then J = 0 (eq_bot_of_finrank_eq_two, via eq_bot_or_eq_ker; both in Colength.prop2). The proof avoids the page's radicial / non-radicial split and uses neither symmetry, transitivity nor the nilpotence of J: for a point χ, φ_χ(a ⊗ b) = χ(a)b sends δx to x − χ(x), so 𝔪_χ ⊂ Az + φ_χ(J) for every 0 ≠ z ∈ 𝔪_χ; if φ_χ(J) = 0 for some χ, 𝔪_χ is a simple module, of dimension 1; otherwise 𝔪_χ ⊂ φ_χ(J) for all χ, and f ≡ δ(φ_χ f) mod 𝔪_χΔ gives Δ ⊂ J + 𝔪Δ at every maximal 𝔪, so Δ ⊂ J by Nakayama and exactness forces A = k. The page's count n = 2 and its unfinished dim V = 1 are both instances of the first case. Not formalised: the reduction from A local artinian to A = k (𝔪A′ = 𝔪, then Prop. 3 and Cor. 2 of N° 1), and the passage from ideals to closed subschemes. Added 2026-10-25: In full, with the reduction from A local artinian to A = k now formalised (namespace ColengthLocal). For A noetherian, 𝔪 maximal, A′ finite over A with every maximal ideal the kernel of an A-algebra map A′ → A/𝔪 (so A local artinian with trivial residue extensions), and J ⊂ Δ an ideal of A′ ⊗_A A′ with A → A′ ⇉ (A′ ⊗_A A′)/J exact and Δ ⊂ (δx) + J for every x ∉ A: 𝔪A′ lies in A (map_le_range), A′/A has Module.length ≤ 1 (colength_le_one), and if J is an equivalence ideal and the length is 1 then J = 0 (eq_bot_of_length_eq_one; all three in prop2 for A local noetherian). Prop. 1 (i) holds over any commutative ring: if A ⊂ A′ and A′/A ≅ A/𝔪, the equivalence ideals of A′ ⊗_A A′ are exactly 0 and Δ (prop1_i). The proof does not use the page's radicial / non-radicial split nor the nilpotence of J: 𝔪A′ ⊂ A because x ∈ 𝔪A′ \\ A would give δx ∈ 𝔫Δ at every maximal 𝔫 of A′, hence Δ ⊂ J by Nakayama, against exactness; the field-case argument then runs in A′/𝔪A′ through φ_χ(a ⊗ b) = χ(a)b̄; and the reduction Θ : A′ ⊗_A A′ → A′₀ ⊗_k A′₀ is injective on Δ (Prop. 3 of N° 1 for I = 𝔪, since every element of 𝔪(A′ ⊗ A′) is 1 ⊗ μ(t)) and carries equivalence ideals to equivalence ideals (Cor. 2, in the direction used). Still not formalised: Prop. 3 and Cor. 2 of N° 1 for a general nilpotent conductor ideal, Prop. 1 (ii) and (iii), and the passage from ideals to closed subschemes." },
    literature: [],
    status: 'unsearched',
    settle:
      'Read D. Ferrand and J.-P. Olivier, « Homomorphismes minimaux d’anneaux », J. Algebra 16 (1970), Th. 2.2–2.3: colength one here is a minimal extension with crucial ideal 𝔪 = conductor, and the two-relation statement may be a reformulation of their decomposed/ramified/inert classification. Then check the later minimal/FCP-extension literature (Dobbs–Shapiro; Picavet and Picavet-L’Hermitte) for a criterion phrased through T ⊗_R T and the diagonal ideal. Page 31, in another hand (the « Notes Murre-Levelt » of the title), treats the same k ⊕ V case; if those notes are identified in print, check them too.',
