    status: 'candidate',
    settle:
      "Second pass 2026-10-26 (Opus 5.5) with alphaXiv (discover_papers twice; answer_pdf_queries on 2402.10676, 2401.00176, 1309.2326, 2405.07092; failed on Breda d'Azevedo et al. by title and URL, timed out on Zvonkin's labri survey), Consensus search (found Breda d'Azevedo et al. 2015 abstract, Jones–Wolfart 2016 table of contents; second query rate-limited), WebSearch (extended), WebFetch, curl + pdftotext/pdftoppm, Crossref and OpenAlex APIs. New reading: El Marraki–Zvonkin 1995, read from page images, pp. 8–9, 12–14. It is the earliest and closest printed source found. Th. 5.1 (every planar hypercarte is H_f, f unique up to homography), Rem. 7.2 (the three distinguished elements of H_f may be chosen arbitrarily and sent to 0, 1, ∞ by a homography) and §7.2–7.3 (the composite f∘g copies H_f into each triangle of H_g, independent of the triangle chosen through ○, □, △) together give the substance of the folder's converse for hypermaps. Also read and not bearing: Pisanski–Williams–Berman 2017 (flag-graph operations, no Belyi), Van den Camp–McKay 2024 (lopsp definition, no Belyi), He–Read 2013/2015, Amburg–Kovaleva 2024 (one composition example, g = 4y/(y+1)², from Zvonkin 2010), Adrianov–Shabat 2023. Still not read, and still blocked: Breda d'Azevedo–Catalano–Karabáš–Nedela 2015 (open archive, but ScienceDirect 403 and no repository copy; the most likely place for a general statement about operations preserving the surface), Magot–Zvonkin 2000, Zvonkin 2010/2011 and his labri survey (server down), Lando–Zvonkin 2004, Jones–Wolfart 2016. Nothing found on the exceptional case (1 a leaf of A). Closest in print: El Marraki–Zvonkin, Sém. Lothar. Combin. 34 (1995), Th. 5.1 (p. 9) with Rem. 7.2 and §7.2–7.3 (pp. 13–14): every planar hypercarte H with any three distinguished elements (vertex, edge midpoint or face centre) is H_f for a Belyi f sending those elements to 0, 1, ∞, so f(0), f(1), f(∞) ∈ {0, 1, ∞}. Composition with f then copies H into every triangle of the canonical triangulation of H_g, whichever triangle through ○, □, △ is used. How it differs: the paper goes from f, or from a hypercarte drawn as H_f, to the operation. It never defines an operation as the pull-back of an arbitrary subset A of the reference sphere and never states that every such operation is a composition. It treats hypermaps, not the folder's clean version (ramification 2 over 1, with vertices, face centres and edge midpoints of A chosen by the rules of p. 124); Rem. 4.3 only says that a pure f gives a map. It has nothing on the exceptional case where 1 is a leaf of A. Combining Th. 5.1, Rem. 7.2 and §7.3 gives the folder's converse in a few lines, but that step is not printed. Wood 2006 §3.2 and Ellenberg 2000/2002 pp. 4–5 give only the forward construction, and later.",
