    settle:
      "Searched 2026-10-25 with WebSearch, WebFetch, curl and pdftotext. Read in full: Kollár arXiv 0910.1076v2. Searched in full text: Kollár, Families of varieties of general type (author's PDF), and EGA IV_2 and IV_3 on numdam (5.12.8, 15.2.2-15.3.4). Read only through its zbMATH review: Kollár, Flatness criteria (1995). Searched the Stacks Project source (git clone) for Hironaka, 15.2.3 and universally open/reduced: no Hironaka-type normality statement there. Searched Kollár, Hulls and husks (arXiv 0805.0576 v1 and v3): it never mentions Hironaka. Web search for Hironaka-type flatness over a reduced base found nothing more. In print: Hironaka over a regular base (1958), over a normal excellent base (Kollár 1995; Kollár 2023, Th. 10.72), the reduced-base flatness under geometrically reduced fibres (EGA IV 15.2.3, 15.3.1), and over a reduced base only under extra hypotheses (proper, flat with normal fibres outside codimension 2, rational singularities: Kollár 2011, Cor. 22 and Rem. 23). Not found: the folder's form over a reduced base with geometrically normal formal fibres, where universal openness and equidimensionality replace normality of the base and the fibre is only reduced at the generic points of x with geometrically normal reduction. Remaining: read Hironaka 1958 and Kollár 1995 in full; Kollár, Rational curves on algebraic varieties (1996), Ch. I.6 (Chow over seminormal bases); Chiang-Hsieh-Lipman 2006. The page-12 sketch rests on uncertain readings and on the unproved Cor. 1.6-1.7. Closest in print: Kollár, Families of varieties of general type (2023), Th. 10.72, and Kollár, Flatness criteria (1995), as summarised by zbMATH: the same conclusion (flat with normal fibres) from the same fibre hypotheses, but the base must be normal and excellent, with X S_2 or without embedded points, and f of pure relative dimension. The folder allows a reduced, non-normal base with geometrically normal formal fibres. It takes universal openness at x as a hypothesis, which is automatic over a normal base by Chevalley (EGA IV 14.4.1), and concludes about U_red with no hypothesis on X. EGA IV 15.2.3 and 15.3.1 do work over a reduced base, but they assume a geometrically reduced fibre and conclude only flatness.",
