    ours:
      "The reading supplies the realisation of the D_∞-set of repères as the flags of the polygons P_a, with ε(σ₀) = ε(σ₁) = −1; the justification that gluing is unique (orientation-reversing homeomorphisms realising a given anti-isomorphism form a contractible space); and the correction of « restriction de ℤ_a/r à ℤ_a/r′ » to restriction to the subgroup of order r′. The transcription has \\\\ill{} words inside the definition of uniform over-marking (batch 3, « surmarquage uniforme, \\\\ill{} de multiplic. par d(a) ») and in the sentence reducing gluing to r(a) = r(a′) (« uniformes \\\\ill{} \\\\ill{} »); the operations themselves are legible. Precise statement fixed by the edition: Let a, a′ be oriented circles each carrying r ≥ 1 marked points, the marked points of a forming a torsor R_a under ℤ/r, and let u : R_a → R_{a′} be an anti-isomorphism of torsors. Then the space of orientation-reversing homeomorphisms a → a′ inducing u on marked points is non-empty and contractible, so the surface glued along a and a′ is defined up to an isomorphism unique up to isotopy. To fix the statement the edition takes from pp. 55–58 the one assertion that is a statement, uniqueness of gluing, rather than the groupoid, which is a definition. It requires r ≥ 1. For r(a) = 0 the page’s torsor is under ℤ_a/0 = ℤ, presumably a torsor of framings, and gluing is unique only once such a ℤ-torsor is given; with the bare circle the ambiguity is a Dehn twist. Proof: on each arc between consecutive marked points the homeomorphisms form a convex set, contractible by the straight-line homotopy. Lean estimate: about 400–800 lines on mathlib’s circle and homeomorphisms.",
