      'For a position D of an added pseudoline passing through ν ≥ 2 vertices of Σ, the stable generisations of D are the 2^ν vertices of a ν-cube whose ν·2^(ν−1) edges are the generisations through exactly one vertex, incidence being specialisation — whereas a straight line through ν vertices of a line arrangement has only 2ν stable perturbations.',
    basis:
      'Page 153 states it in words (« Les générisations stables de D correspondent aux sommets d’un cube de dimension ν, dont les arêtes sont les générisations sous-stables ») and gives the two counts 2^ν and ν2^(ν−1). The exponent of the first 2 is overwritten in the transcription; the counts fix it.',
    ours:
      "The justification — the side on which D is unstuck at each vertex can be chosen independently, two vertices of D never lying on a common pseudoline of Σ — is the reading’s; the page gives none. The contrast with straight lines (2ν sectors around a point of the dual plane on ν dual lines) is this pass’s, and so is the observation that the cube is in tension with page 150, which gives a vertex ρ of the surface X* of positions the order 2·card S_ρ: the two counts of incident sub-stable positions, 2ν and ν·2^(ν−1), agree only for ν = 2, so for ν ≥ 3 the positions near D do not form a surface vertex if all of them are kept. Neither the reading nor the page draws that consequence. Precise statement fixed by the edition: Let Σ be an arrangement of pseudolines (a rank-3 oriented matroid) and let the positions of an added pseudoline be the cocircuit signatures σ that are valid on every line of Σ. Valid means that along each line the signs are of the form + … + (0) − … − up to reversal, with at most one 0 and not all zero (Las Vergnas’s criterion). If σ_D vanishes on exactly ν ≥ 2 vertices, the positions σ ≥ σ_D (σ = σ_D wherever σ_D ≠ 0) are exactly the 2^{ν} sign choices on these vertices with a 0 allowed on any subset; they form the face poset of a ν-cube. The 2^{ν} stable ones (no zero) are its vertices and the ν·2^{ν−1} with exactly one zero its edges. To fix the statement the edition defines positions combinatorially, by Las Vergnas’s localisations checked line by line, rather than as topological pseudolines; the page’s « unsticking » at each vertex is a choice of sign. With that definition the independence argument of the reading becomes exact: two vertices of D never lie on a common line of Σ, so each line carries at most one zero of σ_D, and every choice of signs at those zeros is valid. Lean estimate: about 300–600 lines of finite combinatorics, with the definitions written from scratch, since mathlib has no oriented matroids. With topological pseudolines instead, one would also need the topological representation theorem (Folkman–Lawrence).",
