    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder155.lean', found: "Proved without completion, for K a commutative k-algebra and ι : K → A a k-algebra map into a ring, not central. For a right ideal L of a ring S, the idealiser 𝕀(L) = {λ | λL ⊆ L} is a subring containing L in which L is two-sided, so 𝕀(L)/L is a ring, and it is the largest such subring. 𝒥 = J·(A ⊗_k A), J = Ker(K ⊗_k K → K), is the right ideal generated by the ι(x) ⊗ 1 − 1 ⊗ ι(x) (bigJ_eq, through mathlib's KaehlerDifferential.submodule_span_range_eq_ideal); (A ⊗_k A)/𝒥 ≅ A ⊗_K A, A a K-module through ι on the left (φ_surjective, φ_eq_zero_iff); and λ ∈ 𝒥̃ iff its image Σ aᵢ ⊗ bᵢ satisfies Σ aᵢι(x) ⊗ bᵢ = Σ aᵢ ⊗ bᵢι(x) for all x (mem_idealiser_iff_takeuchi), so 𝒥̃/𝒥 is Takeuchi's A ×_K A (takeuchi_iff). The proof does not go through I(L)/L ≅ End_S(S/L). Not formalised: completed tensor products of pro-objects, the comultiplication." },
