      "Searched 2026-10-25 (Opus 5.5) with WebSearch, the arXiv API (all/abs searches on 'totally aspheric(al)', 'finite category' + filtered/cofiltered + contractible/simply connected/presheaf, compact sifted idempotent), zbMATH Open API (finite category cofiltered aspheric; totally aspheric category; finite filtered category idempotent — no relevant hit, two queries errored), the Stack Exchange API on MathOverflow (finite category filtered aspherical; finite sifted filtered), and full-text grep of three downloaded sources: Maltsiniotis Ast. 301 (2020 augmented version), Cisinski Ast. 308, and Pursuing Stacks (arXiv:2111.01000). Maltsiniotis arXiv:0912.2432 (flagged by the earlier search) was downloaded and checked: it is on asphericity structures and smooth functors, with nothing on filtered or finite categories. Found: definitions and standard properties of total asphericity, the implication filtered ⇒ aspheric, and an MO remark that finite filtered categories have a final idempotent; not found: any statement that a finite (connected) category all of whose connected presheaves have 1-connected (or contractible) categories of elements is cofiltered, nor the edition's doubling lemma ((H) ⇒ pseudo-cofiltrant). Remains: check Lurie HTT §5.3.1 (characterisation of filtered ∞-categories by weak contractibility of C_{p/} for finite p; Kerodon fetch timed out) and the literature on finite filtered/sifted categories (e.g. Adámek–Rosický–Vitale, Algebraic theories, 2011) for a finite-category converse; a person should also verify the edition's added connectedness hypothesis. Closest in print: Standard results go the other way: a filtered (cofiltered) category is aspheric, so for X cofiltered every connected presheaf has contractible category of elements (Maltsiniotis Ast. 301, Prop. 2.4.9, applied to the cofiltered X/F). The converse in the finding uses finiteness and only 1-connectedness of all connected presheaves. Nearby printed conditions are weaker and do not imply cofilteredness for finite categories: total asphericity/siftedness concerns only products of representables, and MO 429437 notes that the finite reflexive-coequalizer shape is sifted but not filtered. Lurie's criterion (cocone categories weakly contractible for every finite diagram) is a different hypothesis that holds for every category, finite or not; it is cited from memory and was not read.",
