      "Searched 2026-10-25 with WebSearch (two queries on formally étale monomorphism / limit preserving / open immersion / effective formal objects), Stacks Project LaTeX sources (artin.tex, criteria.tex, spaces-limits.tex, bootstrap.tex grepped for 'open immersion', 'monomorphism', 'formally'), Hall–Rydh arXiv:1306.4599 full text, Artin Publ. IHÉS 36 (1969) full text from numdam. Not found as stated (functor-level: fpqc sheaves commuting with filtered colimits and adic limits of artinian rings, formally étale monomorphism ⇒ open immersion, locally noetherian base). Closest is Artin 1973 VII.7.2 as quoted in Hall–Rydh Thm 10.3, which yields the claim for excellent S by base change to T and Tag 025F. Remains unread: Artin, Algebraization of formal moduli I (1969, Kodaira volume; no accessible copy found, mathnet.ru Russian translation unreachable — 503/connection reset), Artin, Versal deformations and algebraic stacks (Invent. Math. 1974) §5, and Artin 1973 (Montréal notes) VII.7.2 itself; any of these may contain the statement as a lemma. Closest in print: Hall–Rydh, Theorem 10.3 (attributed to Artin 1973, VII.7.2): a limit-preserving étale stack X over an excellent S with X(B) → X(B/m) bijective for complete local noetherian B is étale over S. Applied to G_T ⊂ T it gives the open-immersion conclusion, but it requires S excellent (the finding needs only locally noetherian, the monomorphism plus fpqc descent along O → Ô replacing approximation), it is phrased as an étaleness criterion over the base rather than an open-immersion criterion for a formally étale monomorphism of functors, and the finding is only obtained after base change and the scheme-level Tag 025F.",
