    claim:
      'For A noetherian with every integral quotient generically normal, and F left exact, commuting with filtered colimits, satisfying the local completion condition and local finiteness, the condition « F restricted to some non-empty open of each V(p) is representable by a finite module » is equivalent to the constructibility of p ↦ dim_{k(p)} F(k(p)).',
    basis:
      'Remarque 5 bis (p. 7) states the equivalence, calls (v) ⇒ (v bis) trivial, and sketches the converse: free lattices P_p of constant rank on an open U, placed in one finite module N, coincide with N_p in codimension 1 off a finite set, hence everywhere on a normal U.',
    ours:
      "The converse rests on the middle of p. 7, which is largely illegible: how N is obtained and why codimension-1 coincidence fails only finitely often are in \\\\ill passages, « codimension » and « normal » are \\\\uncertain, and « Nor(B) » in the hypothesis is an \\\\uncertain reading taken by the edition as the normal locus. The reading gives the structure of the converse, not a proof, and names the final step Hartogs extension. Precise statement fixed by the edition: Let A be noetherian with every integral quotient generically normal, and F : Mod(A) → Ab left exact, commuting with filtered colimits, satisfying the m_p-adic completion condition on finitely generated Â_p-modules and F(M) finitely generated for M of finite length over A_p. Then « for every prime p there is g ∉ p with F restricted to (A/p)_g representable by a finitely generated module » is equivalent to the constructibility of p ↦ dim_{k(p)} F(k(p)). To fix the statement the edition took the hypotheses as written on p. 7, « en présence de (i) à (iv) », with local finiteness only; so fixed, the converse is false. Counterexample (this pass’s): A = k[x, y], k infinite, distinct a_j ∈ k, h_j = x − a_j, F(M) = colim(M →h₂ M →h₃ M → …), i.e. colim_i Hom(A·h₁⋯h_i, M). F is left exact and commutes with filtered colimits. Over each Â_p all but at most one h_j are units, so F ≅ id there and the completion and local-finiteness conditions hold. Every fibre has dim F(k(p)) = 1, a constructible function. But F(A_g) is a strictly increasing union of g_i⁻¹A_g for every g ≠ 0, not finitely generated, so F is representable on no non-empty open. The sketch on p. 7 uses « la finitude de F(N) », a global finiteness that this F violates, since F(A) is not finitely generated. With global finiteness added, as in Th. 12 (iv), the converse is open for the edition. 2026-10-25: the counterexample is checked hypothesis by hypothesis and formalised in lean/Grothendieck/Folder94.lean (constant fibre rank 1, F ≅ id over every A_𝔭, F(A_g) not finite for every g ≠ 0), so the converse with local finiteness only is false. With global finiteness the converse holds, and without the normal-locus hypothesis: if F((A/𝔭)) is finite, on an open D(g) of V(𝔭) where the rank is n the stalks P_q (Prop. 4) are free of rank n, F(B) is finite projective for B = (A/𝔭)_g, and F(B) ⊗_B M → F(M), an isomorphism at every q since F commutes with localisation, shows F_B = Hom(F(B)^∨, −).",
    literature: [],
    status: 'refuted',
    settle:
      "Settled on 2026-10-25. As fixed by the edition (local finiteness only) the converse of Rem. 5 bis is false: F(M) = colim(M →h₂ M →h₃ M → …) on k[x, y], h_j = x − a_j, satisfies (i)–(iv) of Lemme 5 (F ≅ id over every A_𝔭 and Â_𝔭, since at most one h_j lies in 𝔭), has dim F(k(𝔭)) = 1 everywhere, and F(A_g) is a strictly increasing union of (h₂⋯h_i)⁻¹A_g, not finite, for every g ≠ 0 (only finitely many h_j divide g); the same failure occurs on V(𝔭) for every 𝔭 such as (y) where infinitely many h_j stay non-units on every open, while for 𝔭 ∋ some h_j, or 𝔭 = (x − b), F is representable there. Formalised in lean/Grothendieck/Folder94.lean. With the finiteness the page's sketch actually uses (« la finitude de F(N) »), indeed with only F(A/𝔭) finite for every prime 𝔭, the converse holds for every noetherian A, with no normality hypothesis and no Hartogs step: constructibility gives an open D(ḡ) of V(𝔭) where the rank is n; on B = (A/𝔭)_g, Prop. 4 (applied to F restricted to B_q-modules, its hypotheses passing to quotients) gives P_q with constant fibre rank n over the reduced B_q, hence free of rank n; F(B) = F(A/𝔭)_g is finite and locally free of rank n, hence projective; the natural map F(B) ⊗_B M → F(M) localises, since F commutes with filtered colimits, to the isomorphism B_q^n ⊗ M_q → M_q^n, so F_B ≅ F(B) ⊗ − = Hom(F(B)^∨, −). Consequence for 94-representability-criterion: Th. 12 needs Rem. 5 bis only in this global form, so its normal-locus hypothesis (and the assumption A excellent made to fix that finding) is not needed at that step; the argument rests on Prop. 4, whose proof is the edition's. Not formalised: this positive argument, and Prop. 4.",
    reach: 'imprecise',
    onPage: 'partial',
    lean: { file: "lean/Grothendieck/Folder94.lean", found: "For A = k[x, y] (k a field, written k[X][X]), distinct a_j and h_j = x − a_j, the telescope F(M) = colim(M →h M →h M → ⋯) satisfies every hypothesis of Rem. 5 bis with local finiteness and violates its conclusion: at every prime 𝔭 all but at most one h_j are units of A_𝔭, so from some stage F(M) ≅ M for every A_𝔭-module (tele_atPrime_bijective), whence the completion and local-finiteness conditions; dim_K F(K) = 1 for every field K receiving A, in particular every k(𝔭) (finrank_tele_field); and for every g ≠ 0, F(A_g) is not a finite A_g-module (not_finite_tele_away), so F on A_g-modules is Hom(Q, −) for no finite Q (not_representable_away) and (v) fails at 𝔭 = 0. General tools: a telescope whose maps are eventually units is its stage (teleOf_bijective), and a finite telescope of a ring along non-zero-divisors has eventually invertible maps (exists_isUnit_of_finite). Left exactness and commutation with filtered colimits (standard for a filtered colimit of copies of Hom(A, −)) are not restated; the converse under global finiteness is argued, not formalised. Added 2026-10-26: the two commutative-algebra steps of the converse under global finiteness are formalised. (1) Over a reduced local ring B, a finite module P whose rank at every minimal prime equals its fibre rank at the closed point is free, with no noetherian hypothesis (free_of_finrank_eq_of_isReduced): a basis of k ⊗ P lifts to a surjection Bⁿ → P, bijective at each minimal prime by dimension count, so a kernel element has nilpotent, hence zero, coordinates. This is the step « P_q of constant fibre rank n over the reduced B_q, hence free ». (2) If P is finitely presented with free localisations at the maximal ideals and φ : P ⊗ M → N is bijective at every maximal ideal, then P is projective and N ≅ Hom(P^∨, M) (nonempty_equiv_hom_dual), the final step with P = F(B). Still not formalised: Prop. 4 (stalk representability via pro-representability and Matlis duality) and the passage from the functor F to these module statements (identifying F(B)_q with P_q, naturality and localisation of F(B) ⊗ M → F(M)), so the converse itself remains argued, not formalised." },
