    claim:
      'Norms can be axiomatised in any category with finite sums: a class 𝒩 of morphisms stable under identities, composition, base change and sums, closed under the quotients (X^n_Y)/H for H ⊂ 𝔖_n; a multiplicative, additive, base-change-invariant degree with deg((X^n_Y)/H → Y) = card(E^n/H); and canonical sections Can_{X/Y} : Y → Sym^r_Y(X) satisfying identity, transitivity, base change and additivity. G-sets with fibres of constant cardinality, finite locally free morphisms of schemes, and finite unramified coverings are proposed as models.',
    basis:
      'Page 10 states the axioms (i)–(v), (i bis)–(v bis) and (i ter)–(iv ter), with the transitivity diagram on page 11. Page 11 verifies Example I (G-sets), the bottom of the right-hand leaf being partly outside the image and several words \\ill{}. Page 12 asserts Example II, saying that the axioms are verified « très facilement » and giving no verification. Page 13 sketches Example III through Φ(E) = X ×^Γ E: it takes as hypotheses that the quotients X/H exist and that X/H′ under the finite group H/H′ is « non ramifié », so that X/H′ → X/H is in 𝒩, and it supposes (« on suppose ») that Φ takes morphisms of degree r to morphisms of degree r; a struck, boxed parenthesis on that page is still partly \\ill{}, and the page states that Φ takes canonical morphisms to canonical ones (« canoniques » \\uncertain{}) and breaks off without proving it. The end of axiom (iv) is \\ill{}, and the end of (v) is \\ill{} and circled in red with a « ? ».',
    ours:
      'The completion of (v), that the quotient lies in 𝒩, is the reading’s. So are the reading of « conditions (i) à (iv) » as (i ter)–(iv ter) in Example II, the permutation-module argument that verifies Example II, and the reading of « non ramifié » in Example III as Γ acting freely and properly, together with the check that Φ then preserves degrees, which the page only supposes. The transitivity of norms on pages 6–8 is stated « Je dis que », without proof, and the reading derives it from (ii ter). Nothing in the folder proves that any example satisfies (ii ter).',
    literature: [],
    status: 'unsearched',
    settle:
      'Look for an axiomatic framework of « categories with norms » defined by canonical sections into symmetric powers, as opposed to the construction for finite locally free morphisms alone. For that construction: SGA 4 XVII 6.3 (Deligne, trace and norm through the symmetric power); D. Ferrand, « Un foncteur norme », Bull. SMF 126 (1998); D. Rydh on Γ^n, the Hilbert–Chow morphism and families of cycles. For axiomatic transfer and norm structures that cover G-sets: Tambara, « On multiplicative transfer » (1993), and Bachmann–Hoyois, « Norms in motivic homotopy theory » (2017–2021), which treat finite étale maps rather than finite locally free ones. All are cited from memory except Ferrand and Gille et al., « The norm functor over schemes » (arXiv 2401.15051), whose abstracts alone came back from a web search on 2026-10-10. Nothing was read, which is why the status stays unsearched. If one of them states these axioms with these models, mark matched. If the framework turns out to be only the finite locally free construction restated, drop the entry.',
    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder98.lean', found: "In Example I the axioms all hold, including (ii ter), which nothing in the folder verifies for any example. Formalised for maps of types with fibres of constant finite cardinality r (the action of G only has to be respected): the class is closed under identities, composition, base change, sums and the quotients (X^n_Y)/H, with deg(vu) = deg v · deg u, additivity, invariance under base change, and the fibre of (X^n_Y)/H over y in bijection with E^n/H, so its degree is card(E^n/H). With Sym^r_Y(X) the pairs (y, m), m a multiset of r points of u⁻¹(y), and Can_{X/Y}(y) the multiset of the fibre, (i ter), (iii ter), (iv ter) hold, and (ii ter), the diagram of p. 11, holds because the fibre of vu over z is the disjoint union of the fibres of u over the s points of the fibre of v; Can is G-equivariant when u is. The group plays no role beyond that. Example II (finite locally free morphisms), Example III (coverings) and the transitivity of norms of pp. 6–8 are not formalised. Added 2026-10-26: Example III (page 13) now holds in Lean, with « non ramifié » read as every point of X having a neighbourhood disjoint from its Γ-translates. For Φ(E) = X ×^Γ E and any equivariant map f of discrete Γ-sets, Φ(f) is a covering map (IsCoveringMap), whatever the fibres of f. If f has degree r, so does Φ(f): this is the hypothesis the page only supposes, and it needs only a free action. Φ commutes with sums. Sym^r_F(E) is a Γ-set and Can_{E/F} is equivariant. [x, (e', m)] ↦ ([x, e'], [x, m]) is a bijection Φ(Sym^r_F E) ≅ Sym^r_{ΦF}(ΦE) that sends Φ(Can_{E/F}) to Can_{ΦE/ΦF}, which is the claim the page breaks off on. Not formalised: that X/H′ → X/H is a Galois covering with group H/H′ (the identification X/H ≅ Φ(Γ/H)); the axioms for the image of Φ in the category of spaces; Example II; and the transitivity of norms of pp. 6–8." },
