      'Over an arbitrary ring (or base scheme), pinned non-degenerate realisations of the universal regular n-carte by strict pseudo-reflections on a locally free module of rank n+2 are rigid and classified by 2n+1 parameters (λ₀, β₀, λ₁, …, βₙ₋₁, λₙ), the λᵢ invertible and the βᵢ arbitrary, with an explicit criterion (A) ⇔ (B) for a start vector s₀ to exist.',
    basis:
      'Pages 96–100 prove the equivalence of the commutation-and-freeness conditions (A) with the pairing conditions (B), including νₙ = ⟨s₀,a′₀⟩⟨a₀,a′₁⟩⋯⟨aₙ₋₁,a′ₙ⟩ invertible; pages 91–93 give the universal coordinates; pages 102–105 prove that a start vector exists locally under conditions a), a′), b), c). The page-13 margin announces the 2n+1-parameter classification without proof.',
    ours:
      'The classification as stated is the reading’s consequence of the theorem and the universal coordinates (footnote at page 93: conditions (B) hold for all parameters); the page never puts the two together. The reading also corrects the index in (14), reconciles the two inconsistent formulas for ⟨aᵢ₊₁,a′ᵢ⟩ on page 93, redoes the proof of (24) on page 101, and adds the remark that local existence of s₀ holds even over 𝔽₂. Freeness is read in the strong sense (direct-summand basis) that page 103 makes explicit only late.',
    literature: [
      "Danciger, Guéritaud, Kassel, Lee, Marquis, « Convex cocompactness for Coxeter groups », arXiv:2102.02757 — §1.2 (p. 4–5) and §3.1 around Definition 3.3 (read in full text): over ℝ, for representations of a Coxeter group sending generators to reflections (α_i, v_i), the reflections determine the Cartan matrix (α_i(v_j)) up to conjugation by a diagonal matrix, its equivalence class is determined by ρ, and Vinberg [Vi1, Th. 1 & Prop. 6, 13, 17] characterises the discrete reflection representations by conditions on this matrix. Field ℝ, involutive reflections, Coxeter relations on consecutive pairs; no base ring, no pseudo-reflections with arbitrary eigenvalue, no start vector s₀ and no criterion like νₙ invertible.",
      "Hu, « Reflection representations of Coxeter groups and homology of Coxeter graphs », arXiv:2306.12846 (Algebr. Represent. Theory, 2023) — read the setting in the full text: base field ℂ (l. « The base field in this article is assumed to be C »), Coxeter groups, generalized geometric representations classified by characters of integral homology of graphs. Field case, Coxeter relations, no start vector.",
      "Monson & Schulte, « Reflection groups and polytopes over finite fields, III », arXiv:0707.4007 (Adv. Appl. Math.) — §1 (full text read by grep): string Coxeter groups [p₁,…,p_{n−1}] reduced mod p act by orthogonal reflections for a symmetric bilinear form given by a Gram matrix of a basic system; base vertex via Wythoff. Orthogonal reflections over 𝔽_p only; no general pseudo-reflections over a ring, no classification of pinned realisations.",
      "Squier, « Matrix representations of Artin groups », Proc. AMS 103 (1988) 49–53 — §1–2 (full text read): one specific representation of Artin groups by unitary reflections over ℤ[s^±,t^±]; no classification.",
      "Pilkington, « On reflection representations of Coxeter groups over non-commutative rings », arXiv:2107.01530 (2021) — introduction (read): surveys reflection representations and root systems over commutative rings (Hée, Dyer 2009 J. Algebra 321 « Embeddings of root systems I: root systems over commutative rings ») and Dyer's non-commutative Cartan matrices; all for Coxeter groups (involutive generators with Chebyshev-type rank-2 conditions). Dyer 2009 itself not read (ScienceDirect inaccessible).",
      'Web search (2026-10-10), result summaries only, no source read: secondary accounts of Vinberg 1971 (e.g. arXiv 2102.02757, « Convex cocompactness for Coxeter groups ») report that a Cartan matrix is determined up to diagonal conjugation and that cyclic products classify semisimple linear Coxeter groups up to conjugacy. For a string diagram there are no cycles, so over ℝ and for reflections the invariants reduce to the products βᵢ — which would make the field case a match; the summaries say nothing of rings, pseudo-reflections, the degenerate case βᵢ = 0 or the start vector.',
    ],
    status: 'candidate',
    settle:
      "Searched 2026-10-25 with WebSearch (standard and extended), WebFetch, curl to arXiv full texts, and the zbMATH Open API (queries: pseudo-reflections/commutative ring/Cartan matrix; reflection representations Coxeter groups commutative rings; linear groups generated by pseudo-reflections rings). Followed the settle field: Vinberg 1971 (Izv. 5, 1083–1119) could not be read — mathnet.ru full text returned 503 / connection reset — so it is known here only through the DGKLM secondary account, which confirms the field/reflection case: Cartan matrix up to diagonal conjugation determines the representation; for a string diagram this leaves the products only. Also looked at Monson–Schulte (finite fields, orthogonal), Hu 2023 (over ℂ), Squier 1988, Pilkington 2021 / Dyer's root systems over rings. Nothing found that treats the group with only non-consecutive commutation relations acting by pseudo-reflections with arbitrary unit eigenvalues λᵢ over a ring or base scheme, pinned by a start vector s₀ fixed by τ₁…τₙ, nor the criterion (A) ⇔ (B) with νₙ = ⟨s₀,a′₀⟩⟨a₀,a′₁⟩⋯⟨aₙ₋₁,a′ₙ⟩ invertible, nor the 2n+1-parameter rigid classification. Remains: read Vinberg 1971 §§1–3 directly (when mathnet is reachable), Dyer 2009 J. Algebra 321, Hée's « Systèmes de racines sur un anneau commutatif totalement ordonné », and McMullen–Schulte ch. 5 / Coxeter's « Regular complex polytopes » (Shephard groups, base vertex fixed by R₁…Rₙ) for the field/unitary case. Closest in print: Vinberg 1971 as reported by Danciger–Guéritaud–Kassel–Lee–Marquis (arXiv:2102.02757, §3.1, Def. 3.3): a real representation by reflections is determined up to conjugacy by the Cartan matrix (α_i(v_j)) up to diagonal conjugation. It differs in being over ℝ, for involutive reflections of a Coxeter group (finite orders on consecutive products), without a pinned start vector, without the freeness criterion νₙ ∈ k*, and without any relative version over a ring or base scheme; the eigenvalue parameters λᵢ and the degenerate βᵢ = 0 case do not appear.",
    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder72.lean', found: "Holds for E free of rank n + 2 over any commutative ring, in the form (Ā) ⇔ (B̄) of the remark of page 102 (condA_iff_condB): « non-consecutive τᵢ commute, τᵢs₀ = s₀ for 1 ≤ i ≤ n, (s₀, …, s_{n+1}) a basis » iff « ⟨aᵢ, a′ⱼ⟩ = 0 for i, j non-consecutive, ⟨s₀, a′ᵢ⟩ = 0 for 1 ≤ i ≤ n, (s₀, a₀, …, aₙ) a basis, νₙ a unit ». The strictness of the a′ᵢ (« partout non nulles ») is not needed: under (Ā), s_{i+1} − sᵢ = ⟨sᵢ, a′ᵢ⟩aᵢ in a basis forces ⟨sᵢ, a′ᵢ⟩ to be a unit, which is what the commutation argument uses; under (10), (11), ⟨sᵢ, a′ᵢ⟩ = νᵢ, and both directions are triangular changes of basis with invertible diagonal. The classification (the reading's consequence): a realisation with (B̄), normalised by ⟨s₀, a′₀⟩ = 1 and ⟨a_{i−1}, a′ᵢ⟩ = 1, is isomorphic to the coordinate model with μᵢ = ⟨aᵢ, a′ᵢ⟩, βᵢ = ⟨a_{i+1}, a′ᵢ⟩ by a unique isomorphism fixing s₀ and conjugating the τᵢ (pinned_classification, pinned_rigid); the model satisfies (B̄) for every μ, β (model_condB), τᵢ is invertible iff λᵢ = 1 + μᵢ is a unit (model_isUnit_iff), and a linear map compatible with s₀ and the τᵢ forces equal (μᵢ, βᵢ) (invariants_eq): 2n + 1 parameters, λᵢ units, βᵢ arbitrary. Not formalised: the local existence of s₀ under a), a′), b), c) (pages 102–105), the general « libre » (direct summand) form, a locally free non-free E." },
