    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder78.lean', found: "Existence half only, and it holds in every characteristic p, including p = 2 (where the strip polygon is the square) and p = 0 (infinite order): for α = −2, s_{2m} = (−m, m) and s_{2m+1} = (1 + m, −m), the even vertices on x + y = 0 and the odd ones on x + y = 1, with f_{−2} = (x + y)(x + y − 1); u_{−2}^n = id ⇔ 2p ∣ n (through the closure criterion: F_n(−2) = 0 ⇔ 2p ∣ n), so u has order 2p and the 2p vertices are distinct; three consecutive vertices span (determinant 1) and the linear part has determinant 1. The improper u(x, y) = (1 − y, −x): u^{2m} is the translation by m(1, −1), order 2p, vertices (m, −m) and (m + 1, −m) — on the same two lines — distinct, three consecutive ones spanning, σ₁uσ₁ = u⁻¹, linear part of determinant −1, so not unimodular when p is odd. An affinity of k² is determined by its values at s_0, s_1, s_{−1}, so each automorphism of the combinatorial polygon extends in at most one way. That the improper polygon is the only non-unimodular finite regular polygon is not proved." },
