    status: 'candidate',
    settle:
      'The decisive sources are unread: Y. Ihara, « Schwarzian equations », J. Fac. Sci. Univ. Tokyo IA 21 (1974), 97–118 (Hoshi’s reference for the Schwarzian in characteristic p, his [4]); Y. Hoshi, « Frobenius-projective structures on curves in positive characteristic », Publ. RIMS 56 (2020), 401–430, §2 (pseudo-coordinates, Proposition 2.8); Y. Sugiyama and S. Yasuda, « Belyi’s theorem in characteristic two », Compos. Math. 156 (2020), 325–339, §2. If any of them writes G/12 with Hasse derivatives, or proves rigidity at level p² for p = 2 or 3, mark matched. A finer question that would also settle what is the page’s: whether, for (p, N) = (2, 2), G₀ and the Sugiyama–Yasuda invariant determine each other — an orienting web search turned up no source treating this.',
    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder93.lean', found: "Partly: G₀ = G/12 is defined over any ring and satisfies the cocycle and homography invariance; the functions killed by D¹, D², D³ are the p-th powers for p > 3 and the p²-th powers for p = 2, 3; and the rigidity holds in A[T]/(T^n) with α = 0 over any ring of characteristic p (n = p, and n = p² for p = 2, 3), with no Pic(A) hypothesis — but with a unit determinant, since the page’s ad − bc = 1 fails (s = 2t over 𝔽₅). The reduction from α ≠ 0 to α = 0 is not proved. Added 2026-10-25: The reduction from A[T]/(T^{p^N} − α) to α = 0 in the Proposition of pp. 121–123, which the page makes without argument (« OPS »), holds for every α when A is local (so Pic(A) = 0), in characteristic p with C(k,3) a unit for 3 ≤ k < p^N: N = 1 for p > 3, N = 2 for p = 2, 3. The proof base-changes to B = A[T]/(T^{p^N} − α) itself, where T^{p^N} − α = (T − β)^{p^N}: the Taylor map f ↦ f(β + Y) into B[Y] commutes with the divided derivatives and sends T^{p^N} − α to Y^{p^N}, so the α = 0 rigidity applies over B and gives the osculating homography at β, with coefficient vector v = (a, s − aT, c, 1 − cT) in B⁴, c = −D⁽²⁾s/D⁽¹⁾s, a = D⁽¹⁾s + cs. Differentiating once and twice gives (cY + 1)·θ(vⱼ) ≡ vⱼ mod Y^{p^N}; since (cY + 1)^{p^N} = 1 + c^{p^N}Y^{p^N}, every ℓ^{p^N − 1}vⱼ with ℓ = λc + μ(1 − cT), λ, μ ∈ A, is killed by the Hasse derivatives of orders below p^N, hence lies in A; this holds over any ring of characteristic p (rigidity_alpha). Over a local ring one of c^{p^N} ∈ A and (1 − cT)^{p^N} = 1 − c^{p^N}α is a unit, which makes the denominator ℓ^{p^N − 1} a unit of B. Not formalised: a non-local A with Pic(A) = 0, where the vectors ℓ^{p^N − 1}v should span a rank-one projective module whose freeness is what Pic(A) = 0 would supply. Added 2026-10-26: the reduction from A[T]/(T^{p^N} − α) to α = 0 now holds over every A of characteristic p with Pic(A) = 0, not only local A (rigidity_alpha_pic, with the cases p > 3, p = 2, p = 3 as rigidity_alpha_pic_of_gt, _four, _nine): s = (aT + b)/(cT + d) with a, b, c, d ∈ A and cT + d a unit of B. The proof takes the A-submodule I of B of the x = c'T + d with s·x ∈ A + AT; the Y² coefficient of the Taylor expansion gives c·I ⊆ A and (1 − cT)·I ⊆ A, rigidity_alpha puts c^{q−1} and (1 − cT)^{q−1} in I, and α c^q + (1 − cT)^q = 1, so I·(Ac + A(1 − cT)) = A; I is an invertible submodule, and Pic(A) = 0 makes it A·u with u ∈ B^× (Mathlib's Submodule.ker_unitsToPic). Still not formalised: the sheaf version on a curve (t only a local coordinate), the global injectivity into Conn₀, uniqueness of G₀ up to sign over ℤ, and the characteristic-p uniqueness of Remark 3." },
