    lean: { file: 'lean/Grothendieck/Folder79.lean', found: "Holds in the model over any nonzero commutative ring and any edge type: a map ≠ id commuting with SL₂ moves no vertex to distance 0 or 1, and to distance 2 exactly when it is σ (for Z/n only n = 4); over semi-local rings, so for units μ ≠ ±1 of Z/n, every vertex is at distance exactly 3 from its image. In the abstract coset graph the same holds when no other vertex shares a star; the double-cone step to the octahedron is not proved. Added 2026-10-25: Holds, in a wider setting than the page's. The double-cone lemma of page 275 holds for any connected symmetric graph whose stars all carry a polygon of the same order n ≥ 3 (no orientation, no flag-regularity): two distinct vertices with the same star E force n = 4, S = {s, s′} ∪ E and an isomorphism with the octahedron K_{2,2,2}; the proof reads, in the star of a vertex u of E, that s and s′ have the same polygon neighbours, so are opposite (j = i + 2) and 4 = 0 in Z/n. With it, page 188 holds for a coset graph H/U₀ (σ² ∈ U₀) of this kind, H being transitive on arcs: an H-equivariant permutation λ ≠ 1 moving one vertex to distance ≤ 2 forces the octahedron, and then λ is its antipody (λy ≠ y has the star of y for every y); outside the octahedron the distance is never 0, 1 or 2, so it is exactly 3 when it is ≤ 3. Only arc-transitivity is used, not the homothety condition (70 b) or faithfulness (72). Without equal vertex orders the double pyramid over an n-gon is a counterexample, as the page's own « double pyramide » suggests. Not formalised: the bound ≤ 3 itself (condition a), a hypothesis on the page) outside semi-local rings, and that the octahedral antipody is a homothety in the abstract setting (it is seen in the model over Z/4). Added 2026-10-26: The octahedral antipody is a homothety in the abstract setting. For any graph identified with the octahedron K_{2,2,2}, the antipody α (each vertex to the one other vertex not adjacent to it) is a fixed-point-free involution with the star of each vertex, at distance 2, satisfying (H1) {s, t} ∈ A ⟺ {αs, α⁻¹t} ∈ A, commuting with every injective map that preserves the graph, and it is the only permutation moving each vertex to another vertex with the same star. When the coset graph H/U₀ is the octahedron (no hypothesis on σ), α commutes with H, satisfies (70 b), is ≠ 1 and strict (A_α = A); it fails the faithfulness (72), so it lies in no homothety group of (88). With the double cone, an H-equivariant λ ≠ 1 moving a vertex to distance ≤ 2 is exactly this antipody. Not formalised: the existence of a coset graph isomorphic to the octahedron (e.g. under its rotation group of order 24), for which the model over Z/4 stands in; the bound ≤ 3 outside semi-local rings." },
